1
00:00:00,370 --> 00:00:01,776
Furniture.
Luggage.

2
00:00:01,816 --> 00:00:04,509
Gold. Rice.
Knowledge.

3
00:00:04,549 --> 00:00:05,056
Work.

4
00:00:05,096 --> 00:00:06,247
We’re told
to memorise lists

5
00:00:06,287 --> 00:00:06,928
like these:

6
00:00:06,968 --> 00:00:09,096
no plural,
singular verb.

7
00:00:09,637 --> 00:00:10,948
But what if
you didn’t have

8
00:00:10,988 --> 00:00:12,147
to memorise them?

9
00:00:12,187 --> 00:00:13,516
What if you
could prove it,

10
00:00:13,556 --> 00:00:14,787
with logic?

11
00:00:15,437 --> 00:00:17,271
Case one:
furniture.

12
00:00:17,311 --> 00:00:18,294
Why
is ‘The furniture

13
00:00:18,334 --> 00:00:20,179
are expensive’
wrong?

14
00:00:20,803 --> 00:00:21,531
Proof one.

15
00:00:21,571 --> 00:00:22,761
Point
to a furniture.

16
00:00:22,801 --> 00:00:24,860
This? That’s
a chair.

17
00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:26,554
This? A table.

18
00:00:26,594 --> 00:00:27,870
A sofa. A bed.

19
00:00:27,910 --> 00:00:28,909
Every object
already has its

20
00:00:28,949 --> 00:00:29,985
own name.

21
00:00:30,437 --> 00:00:32,649
Nothing here
is ‘a furniture’.

22
00:00:32,689 --> 00:00:34,387
So furniture
is not the name

23
00:00:34,427 --> 00:00:36,211
of one object.

24
00:00:37,337 --> 00:00:38,135
Proof two.

25
00:00:38,175 --> 00:00:39,329
Furniture
is the name for

26
00:00:39,369 --> 00:00:40,439
the whole group.

27
00:00:40,479 --> 00:00:42,976
Chair,
table, sofa,

28
00:00:43,016 --> 00:00:44,623
bed: one box,
one label.

29
00:00:45,070 --> 00:00:46,539
Try counting
the label:

30
00:00:46,579 --> 00:00:48,547
one furniture,
two furnitures?

31
00:00:48,587 --> 00:00:49,657
It fails.

32
00:00:49,697 --> 00:00:51,216
We count
what’s inside:

33
00:00:51,256 --> 00:00:53,220
one chair,
two chairs.

34
00:00:54,370 --> 00:00:55,288
Proof three.

35
00:00:55,328 --> 00:00:56,745
A group name
works as one

36
00:00:56,785 --> 00:00:58,016
whole, so the verb

37
00:00:58,056 --> 00:00:59,063
is singular:

38
00:00:59,103 --> 00:01:01,200
‘The furniture
is expensive.’

39
00:01:02,337 --> 00:01:03,253
Need a number?

40
00:01:03,293 --> 00:01:05,525
The two won’t
stick to furniture.

41
00:01:05,565 --> 00:01:07,100
Give it something
countable:

42
00:01:07,140 --> 00:01:08,316
‘Two pieces
of furniture

43
00:01:08,356 --> 00:01:09,983
are expensive.’

44
00:01:11,103 --> 00:01:12,677
Now you try:

45
00:01:12,717 --> 00:01:14,047
luggage.
Point to a luggage?

46
00:01:14,087 --> 00:01:15,327
No, that’s
a suitcase,

47
00:01:15,367 --> 00:01:16,296
that’s a bag.

48
00:01:16,336 --> 00:01:17,712
Group name, so:

49
00:01:17,752 --> 00:01:18,965
‘The luggage
is heavy.’

50
00:01:19,903 --> 00:01:21,331
So don’t memorise.

51
00:01:21,371 --> 00:01:23,528
Ask: can I point
to one of it?

52
00:01:23,568 --> 00:01:25,007
If not, it’s
a group name.

53
00:01:25,047 --> 00:01:26,445
Treat it as one.

